  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Thursday...\n\nShortwave energy under westerly flow is currently creating\nconditions favorable for gap winds across the foothills of south\ncentral MT. A tight gradient along with 35-50 kt 700 mb winds is\ncreating a favorable set up for strong gap winds in these areas\n(Livingston, Big Timber, and Harlowton). With the strongest\ngradient favorable for the Livingston area having passed, strong\nwinds are expected to diminish overnight, especially with passage\nof the front earlier this evening. This will then turn the \ngradient and 700 mb winds to be more favorable for areas of Big \nTimber through Judith Gap. Although the strongest winds (50-60 \nmph) are expected to be late morning into the afternoon, there is \na 20-30% chance for strong gusts overnight. Use caution if \ntraveling along I-90 near Big Timber and along HWY 191 through \nJudith Gap. \n\nAs far as precipitation goes, not too much has changed in the\nforecast. The ensembles are still differing on amounts keeping the\nbetter amounts to the south of the region. Precipitation is\noccuring with the frontal passage this evening, mainly across\nsoutheast MT and in the higher terrain. Less than a tenth of an\ninch is likely with these showers. Currently, the \nAbsaroka/Beartooth mountains are expecting 8-16 inches of snow. \nThe Beartooth/Red Lodge foothills can expect to see up to 5 \ninches, highest in the north facing upper foothills. This area is\nexpected to see the best snowfall rates after midnight Friday as \nlow to mid level winds become northerly. Other area mountains such\nas the Bighorns and Crazies can expect to see around 5-10 inches.\nAs snow levels drop late Friday into Saturday to around 4,000 ft,\nmuch of the lower elevations can expect to see a rain/snow mix, \nwith snow along the higher hills south of Billings. The high res- \nmodels have most of the precipitation moving south by 6 am \nSaturday morning with the exception of some lingering light \nmountain snow. \n\nRidging is expected to move in for Sunday, keeping conditions \ndry. As a more west/southwest flow moves in, a favorable gap wind\ngradient (10-15 mb) looks to form across the higher terrain of \nsouth central MT with 700 mb winds of 20-25 kts. This will bring \nabout a 30% chance for gusts to 50 mph in the Livingston/Nye areas\nSunday morning. Weak energy moving in Monday will bring weak \nforcing keeping precipitation mostly confined to the higher \nterrain with some isolated showers possible across the lower \nelevations. \n\nHigh temperatures are expected to be in the 40s to low 50s today,\nslightly cooling to the 30s and 40s for the remainder of the\nperiod. TS",
